 close to us culturally we have had  enormous interaction with Iran I was in  Iran two months ago and I visited Shiraz  and his fahan and everywhere I went I  could sense you know the the muffled  footsteps of history as it were that  link India and Iran together trade Iran  as far as energy security is concerned  is also very  important for us we twelve percent of  our import of crude petroleum comes from  Iran and in recent months because of the  sanctions imposed by the United States  and other European countries apart from  the UN sanctions there have been  difficulties in in effecting payments  for these imports from iran so to sum up  we cannot be entirely divorced from what  happens in Iran today and we have very  close and continuing linkages with Iran  but we've acted with a sense of  responsibility when it comes to Iran's  nuclear program my name is Arjun  Canadian I'm a second year MBA student  at the Yale School of Management one of  the discussions that we constantly have  is with respect to investments in india  and when it comes to venture investing  in specific in india now in the united  states most of venture investing  typically started with dual purpose  investments but unfortunately all sorts  of investment that's been happening in  india and continues to happen is in the  growth sector and there's very little  investing that's happening in the  venture sector now could you speak a  little bit the collaboration that has  been happening between the United States  and the end India when it comes to the  whole science and technology sector and  innovation such well that's the emphasis  I sought to give in my in my speech  today and I think the linkages between  research and R&amp;D institutions so let's  say universities with industry which you  have in this country is not so well  established in India and I think that is  one area where when you talk of  educational collaboration and bringing  in business and industry to be a  partners in this we have a lot to learn  from the United States I mentioned the  health sector is one area where we can  have much more capital coming in and  know how technology innovation for co  development &amp; Co research &amp; Co  innovation between India and the United  States and finally agriculture we talk  of an evergreen revolution to to  supplant the green revolution of the 60s  in India  how do you increase agricultural  productivity how do you deal with  groundwater availability how do you  increase basically productivity and  productivity of crops that can provide  better nutrition to the vast numbers of  people who still live in poverty in  India so education health science and  technology agriculture all these are  areas where I believe we should be  having much more inputs from the United  States I think for political reasons and  for certain degrees of estrangement that  existed in the past for whatever reasons  this had not been happening but the  United States after President Obama's  visit last year announced that it would  be relaxing export controls on high  technology with India and that has begun  to happen with the removal of three of  our major entities from the entities  list and the announcement of removal of  certain export controls in crime control  elements in regional stability related  elements and exports from the United  States to India so we've made a  beginning in these areas and we intend  to build on it there is a bilateral  investment treaty in the works also  which should open up the universe a  little more thank you very much your  excellency yes two days back there was a  column in The Guardian which claimed  that that Indian government was denying  visas and deporting foreign  correspondents trying to cover Kashmir  chattisgarh another so sensitive areas  for instance david bursa me off USA how  do you respond to this column Creole to  identify yourself sorry I'm aniket I'm a  PhD student here Anna Kate from where  from Nasik India from Na